              Not everything in CMDS is a command              ===================================Nearly every file under /dd/CMDS is a 68000 program module.  The restare listed here.  Starting one of them to see if it works proves nothingeither way.TRAP LIBRARIES -- type $0B.  A program links these; you do not run them.    cio         Microware's C library for cc 3.2 and earlier, and what the    math        star in DOC/INDEX marks.  math is software floating point;    math881     math881 uses a 68881 coprocessor.  Shipped by permission.    csl         Ultra C's C Shared Library, and the 68020/030/040 build of    csl020      it.  Shipped by permission.    X11R6shl    X11R6 shared library loader    graph       in CMDS/GAMES: the Atari `Graph' library.  g, striche,                apfel, sine, showpic, graphdemo and graphsave link it.    vmod_trap   in CMDS/COMMS: the trap handler rxmod and txmod call to                enter a module into the module directory.  `load' it                before running either.    cctrap      in CMDS/DRIVERS: the trap handler cpucache calls to read                and switch the cachesSYSTEM MODULES, FILE MANAGERS, DRIVERS AND DESCRIPTORS.  These belong ina system's module directory, loaded or in a bootfile, not on a commandline.For any 68000 machine:    fpu               Microware's floating-point emulation module, for a                      machine with no 68881/68882.  It goes in your                      bootfile and your Init module's extension list.                      DOC/fpu.doc is its document and its distribution                      grant, which asks for that file to stay with it.    ptxm              Path Table eXtension Module: a kernel extension                      that lets user-state processes open unlimited I/O                      paths.  Courtesyware.  It needs supervisor state;                      `ptxminst' installs it.  DOC/ptxm/ptxm.txt is its                      document.  (module name: Ptxm)In CMDS/DRIVERS, each with its source in SRC:    ptyman            PtyMan, a pseudo-terminal file manager.                      DOC/ptyman/ptyman.doc  (module name: PtyMan)    ptydrv            its placeholder driver     (module name: PtyDrv)    pty               its server-side descriptor, /pty    tty               its client-side descriptor, /tty -- not the command                      of that name in CMDS    lfcrman           a file manager showing another device with its                      line endings translated between LF and CR    h0t, thlpct       example lfcrman descriptors: /h0t is /h0, /thlpct                      is /thlpc, translated    disp              an example SCF driver written in C    di                its descriptorFor the MM/1:    keydrv.mm1        keyboard driver          (module name: keydrv)    msdrv.901_340     mouse driver             (module name: msdrv)    msdrv_340.901.ms  mouse driver, variant    (module name: msdrv)    snddrv            sound driver    rb37c65           floppy driver, 37C65 controller    windio.52         windowing terminal driver (module name: windio)    scsi_mm1a         SCSI routines the hard disk driver links to, a                      type $02 subroutine moduleBASIC09 I-CODE -- type $02, language $02.  Compiled BASIC09, run with yourown `runb': `runb bio'.    bio         biorhythm chart    wysetime    Wyse terminal clock-setter    blackjack   Las Vegas blackjack    dedit       disk sector editorStarted as a program, one of these stops with E_BMID (205), `bad moduleID', because OS-9 was asked to fork I-code as machine code.  That readslike damage and is not.  The type/language word at header offset $12tells them apart: $0202 for I-code, $0101 for a 68000 program.DATA MODULES -- type $04.    rtfdat      RTF FORTRAN data module    wn.stb      in CMDS/WN: WN's symbol tableSCRIPTS -- not modules at all.    man         everything this disk has about a program; a ksh script    who         who is logged in; Microware shell syntax    mscheck     mtools disk verifier; a ksh scriptMODULE GROUPS -- one file, several modules.  OS-9 loads the whole groupand keeps it resident until the combined link count reaches zero, so theextra modules are not leftovers.    lfmaker     lfmaker (program) + lfmaker_win (window descriptor)    cyberwar    CyberWar and cyber_game (programs) + cyber_start_win                and cyber_retry_win (window descriptors)    blackjack   blackjack, draw and shuffle, all I-code    dedit       dedit, hex_dec, dec_hex, get_media_size, p_sector,                go_xy, p_sir, e_sector, rd_sector, p_fd and map_sector,                all I-codeA few other files are a byte or two longer than their module: trailingpadding, not a second module.  Count $4AFC headers, not bytes, beforecalling anything a group.NAMES INSIDEA module answers to the name in its header, which is not always itsfilename -- and once resident, it is found by that name.  Most are theauthor's capitalisation: `aterm' is ATerm, `lha' is LHa, `ckermit' iswermit.  `tabs' and `hc' are B_tabs and B_hc, the names they came with.Some are deliberate: `math881' answers to math and `csl020' to csl, so aprogram linking the plain name gets the build for its CPU; the gcc passesinstalled in CMDS carry the names their drivers' originals have (gcc_cc2is cc2).